          ],
          "level": 1,
          "content": "Two Variables, One Line"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.756,
            0.168,
            0.824,
            0.182
          ],
          "content": "0905CH13"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.259,
            0.882,
            0.339
          ],
          "content": "In Chapter 2 of Ganita Manjari, Part I, we explored linear patterns and learnt about linear polynomials. In this chapter, we will go a step further in exploring linear equations in two variables, by learning how to solve a **pair** of linear equations."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.362,
            0.615,
            0.384
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "13.1 Linear Equations in Two Variables"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.395,
            0.518,
            0.53
          ],
          "content": "Radha stops at a fruit cart. The price tags say that mangoes cost ₹60 per kg and bananas cost ₹50 per kg. She buys some kilograms of mangoes and some kilograms of bananas. Altogether, she pays ₹280. Can we express this situation as an equation?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.529,
            0.519,
            0.588
          ],
          "content": "Since we do not know how many kilograms of mangoes or bananas she bought, let us assume that"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.532,
            0.396,
            0.907,
            0.622
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_0_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.592,
            0.518,
            0.632
          ],
          "content": "x = number of kilograms of mangoes"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.636,
            0.546,
            0.656
          ],
          "content": "y = number of kilograms of bananas"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.661,
            0.881,
            0.72
          ],
          "content": "The cost of the mangoes is ₹60x and the cost of the bananas is ₹50y. The total cost is ₹280. Hence, we can express this using the linear equation"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.412,
            0.719,
            0.585,
            0.738
          ],
          "content": "60x + 50y = 280.",
          "image_source": "images/page_0_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.743,
            0.881,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": "Notice that the left-hand side (LHS) and the right-hand side (RHS) of the equation both represent the same quantity, that is, the total cost (in ₹)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.801,
            0.88,
            0.821
          ],
          "content": "The equation $60x + 50y = 280$ is a **linear equation in two variables**."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
